Dropbear's SSH server and OpenSSH's sftp-server, built for the head unit (QNX 6.5.0, armle-v7, GCC 4.4.2) and for
this machine. Third-party code: its sources are two git submodules, and nothing of them is copied into this
repository.

  dropbear/  https://github.com/mkj/dropbear, tag DROPBEAR_2026.94, commit 28216cd9af822732a1549b78621c2ea9a76a0fe5
             (Dropbear 2026.94). MIT, with other free licences for some parts: its LICENSE.
  openssh/   https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable, tag V_10_6_P2, commit d449e662b6b8b8cb20e852d8084c269af1c5e8f1
             (OpenSSH 10.6p2, portable). BSD and licences freer than that: its LICENCE.

Both are shallow (.gitmodules). Ours, beside them:

  dropbear-qnx.patch  changes to Dropbear's source, each said below. MIT, as Dropbear (REUSE.toml)
  localoptions.h      Dropbear's build options, which override its src/default_options.h. MIT, as Dropbear
  build-qnx.sh        configure and make for the head unit, run in the QNX SDP image
  build-host.sh       the same for this machine, with its own compiler
  relay/              sshrelay, the program of the package's channel, which starts the server for each connection
                      (below, "The relay"). Ours, under the repository's licence
  relay/version.hpp   the package's version (below, "The package")
  package/manifest.json.in  the package's manifest, with a placeholder for the version
  ../tools/ssh_pkg.py    make ssh-pkg, make ssh-release; its test is ../tests/test_ssh_pkg.py (make test-ssh-pkg)
  ../tools/check_ssh.py  make ssh-check
  ../tests/ssh/       the relay's tests, make ssh-test


Building

From headunit/:

  make ssh-src     build/ssh/src/: each submodule's pinned commit (the one in the index, which is what
                   `git submodule update` checks out) exported with `git archive`; Dropbear's copy patched
                   (patch -F 0, so a hunk whose context has moved fails) and given localoptions.h. The
                   submodules are fetched first, shallow, when they are not there. They are only read: no build
                   writes into them, and `git status` stays clean.
  make ssh         build/ssh/qnx/dropbearmulti and sftp-server, stripped, for the head unit: build-qnx.sh in the
                   SDP image (QNX_IMAGE, qnx650-sdk when unset). It uses the bare ntoarmv7-gcc driver, which needs
                   no QNX licence file (qcc does), so no licence folder is mounted.
  make ssh-host    build/ssh/host/: the same two programs for this machine, for tests.
  make ssh-relay   build/ssh/qnx/sshrelay, the relay for the head unit, stripped: qcc in the SDP image, as the
                   agent's programs are built, which needs the QNX licence folder. ./build.sh ssh runs it there
                   (below).
  make ssh-relay-host  build/ssh/host/sshrelay, the relay for this machine, with the test hooks.
  make ssh-test    the relay's tests (tests/ssh/run_relay.py), on this machine; needs make ssh-host first.
  make ssh-check   the head unit's two programs are ARM EABI5 soft-float executables, not position-independent,
                   that ask for libc.so.3 and libsocket.so.3 only (no zlib), and its relay the same with
                   libcpp.so.4 too, and without the test hooks; this machine's key tool makes an ed25519 host key,
                   and its server, given one connection on a socket pair (-i), sends the banner
                   SSH-2.0-dropbear_<version>.

  make ssh-pkg     release/pkg/org.nbtapp.ssh-<version>.nbtpkg, not signed (below, "The package").
  make ssh-release KEY=FILE
                   that package signed with the package key, to release/pkg/signed/, and its SHA-256 printed.

  QNX_LICENSE_DIR=/path/to/qnx/license ./build.sh ssh
                   all of it in order: make ssh-host, make ssh-test, make ssh, make ssh-relay in the SDP image,
                   make ssh-check, make ssh-pkg.

make ssh-src writes build/ssh/src/sources: each submodule's pinned commit, and the SHA-256 of the patch and of
localoptions.h. make ssh copies it to build/ssh/qnx/sources with the SHA-256 of build-qnx.sh: what the head unit's
dropbearmulti and sftp-server were built from, which hold no version or build of ours.

`make ssh` and `make ssh-host` each export again and build from the start, outside the export, in
build/ssh/<qnx|host>/obj/, where configure.log and make.log are kept. Everything is under build/, which
`make clean` removes.

What is built:

  dropbearmulti  PROGRAMS="dropbear dropbearkey scp" MULTI=1: the server, its key tool and scp in one program,
                 which acts as the one it is started as (a link's name, or its first argument). dbclient, the
                 client, is not built: the head unit only serves. The server needs nothing of it, and neither
                 does scp as the far end of a client's copy (scp -t, scp -f); only an scp started on the head
                 unit to copy to another machine would. Compression is built out (--disable-zlib): the SDP's zlib
                 is from 2005, and the server then offers "none" only.
  sftp-server    OpenSSH's, `make sftp-server` only, with no change to its source. Dropbear starts it for an
                 sftp session by the bare name sftp-server (localoptions.h), looked up in PATH.

The configure options, for the head unit (build-qnx.sh):

  - CC=ntoarmv7-gcc, CFLAGS -O2 -march=armv7-a -mfpu=vfpv3 -mfloat-abi=softfp -D_QNX_SOURCE -std=gnu99: as the
    agent is built, soft-float calls.
  - LIBS=-lsocket: configure does not find socket() by itself.
  - Dropbear ac_cv_have_decl_htole64=yes: QNX defines htole64 and the like as macros in <net/netbyte.h> (through
    <netinet/in.h>); configure looks only in <endian.h>, misses them, and compat.h's prototypes then collide with
    the macros.
  - Dropbear --disable-harden, OpenSSH --without-hardening --without-stackprotect --without-pie: among those
    flags is -pie, and a position-independent executable does not start on the head unit.
  - --disable-lastlog, -utmp, -utmpx, -wtmp, -wtmpx (and Dropbear's -loginfunc, -pututline, -pututxline): no
    login records are written. --disable-shadow / --without-shadow: logins are by public key only.
  - Dropbear --enable-bundled-libtom: the SDP has no LibTomCrypt or LibTomMath.
  - OpenSSH --without-openssl (sftp-server needs no cryptography), --without-zlib, --disable-strip (the script
    strips).
build-host.sh uses the same options where they apply: not the cross-compiler, CFLAGS, -lsocket, the htole64
answer, or Dropbear's --disable-harden and OpenSSH's three hardening options.

What dropbear-qnx.patch changes, each for QNX 6.5 or for the server run with -i on a socket pair:

  - src/compat.h: O_NOFOLLOW defined as 0 when the system has none; QNX 6.5 has none. Every use but scp's has
    O_EXCL, which refuses an existing link anyway.
  - src/scpmisc.c: a vasprintf() of its own on QNX, whose libc has none.
  - src/sshpty.c: <unix.h> on QNX, which declares openpty() (it is in libc.a).
  - src/netio.c: -i on a socket pair or a pipe, which has no IP address, no longer ends "Failed socket address";
    the address is given as "local", port 0.
  - src/svr-authpubkey.c: with an absolute -D folder, only that folder's ownership and mode are checked, not
    every folder above it (the head unit's /tmp and /dev/shmem are mode 777).
  - src/svr-auth.c (QNX only): with no /etc/shells, an executable login shell is accepted. QNX 6.5's built-in
    getusershell() list lacks /bin/ksh, root's shell ("User 'root' has invalid shell, rejected"), and making
    /etc/shells would be a write to the system partition.
  - src/svr-chansession.c: with -e the PATH the server was started with is kept, where it would otherwise be
    the compiled DEFAULT_ROOT_PATH.

What localoptions.h sets:

  - no default host key or pid file paths (/nonexistent/...): -r is always given, and nothing is written under
    /etc;
  - inetd mode (-i) and the listening mode both built in, no re-exec;
  - public keys only (with authorized_keys options), no password login, no agent or X11 forwarding;
  - no message of the day;
  - DROPBEAR_SVR_DROP_PRIVS 0, and no Unix-socket forwarding either way: QNX 6.5 has no setresgid(), which
    dropping privileges needs (sysoptions.h stops the build otherwise), and Unix-socket forwarding needs the
    dropping. Root logs in as root, so nothing is lost. (DROPBEAR_SVR_MULTIUSER 0 builds, but at run time
    refuses a kernel whose getgroups() works: "Non-multiuser Dropbear requires a non-multiuser kernel".)
  - the head unit's shell PATH as the default PATH, which is used only without -e;
  - SFTPSERVER_PATH "sftp-server", found in PATH.


The package

org.nbtapp.ssh, "SSH access" (tools/ssh_pkg.py; ../README.md, "The SSH package"). Its manifest
(package/manifest.json.in) places, all in the package's own folder ${app}:

  bin/dropbearmulti, bin/sftp-server, bin/sshrelay   the head unit's three programs, mode 0755
  bin/scp                                            a link to dropbearmulti, which acts as scp under that name
  licenses/                                          the licence texts (below, "Licences to ship with the
                                                     programs") and a README that says which program each is of

It has one channel, ssh: for NBTApps itself (apps []), both ways, switched (off until a phone or nbtctl switches
it on), and started on demand: its program is bin/sshrelay. Its logs are the channel's, ${tmp}/channels/ssh.log
and ssh.log.1. It runs no hook, places nothing outside its folders, and asks for no restart, so the one risk flag it
gets is starts-program. It requires agent >=1.7.0-beta.1, the first with channels (a pre-release is outside
>=1.7.0), and app >=0.9.0.

Its version is the relay's: NBT_SSH_VERSION in relay/version.hpp. sshrelay --version prints it with what
git describe --always --dirty said when the relay was built, and the relay's log begins with the same line
(sshrelay 1.0.0-beta.1 (build 03039b4)). make ssh-check finds it in the head unit's relay, and make ssh-release
refuses a relay of another version, or of another commit than the one checked out, or of a checkout with changes;
and Dropbear and sftp-server built from other sources than that commit pins (build/ssh/qnx/sources, above). The
version is raised whenever anything the package holds changes, also when only Dropbear or OpenSSH does: a version
that has been released is never built again with other content.

What it writes on a head unit: the host key, once, in ${data} (hostkey_ed25519, and beside it hostkey_ed25519.pub,
the key tool's copy of the public line, which nothing reads; both kept until the package's data is removed);
everything else on the RAM disk, in ${tmp}: the socket, the keys folders, the state file and, by the agent, the
channel's logs.


The relay

sshrelay (relay/) is the program of the package's channel "ssh". The agent starts it when the channel is opened
and nothing listens (headunit/README.md, "A package's channels"), and connects to it once for each channel a phone
opens. For each connection the relay starts one Dropbear server on a socket pair and carries the bytes between the
channel and the server. One process, one thread, one select loop; nothing in the loop waits.

  relay.hpp, relay.cpp    the loop, a connection from its first byte to its end
  driver.cpp              the pty driver, started on demand
  state.cpp               the state file, and what a start ends of what a relay that died left
  key_lines.hpp, .cpp     the keys message and the rule each key line is held to
  processes.hpp, .cpp     the process list: each process's parent, group, session and start time (QNX: devctl
                          DCMD_PROC_INFO on /proc/<pid>/as; Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat)
  child.hpp, .cpp         starting the server, the key tool and the driver
  main.cpp                the environment, the signals
It takes the agent's JSON reader and writer, its file helpers and its program names as source files (agent/json,
json_writer, fsutil, sha256, programs).

Its start. From the environment the agent gives: NBT_TMP_DIR, NBT_DATA_DIR, NBT_APP_DIR and NBT_CHANNEL; with one
missing it says so on a line and exits with 2. It ignores SIGPIPE. Before it listens, an older relay that the
state file names and that still runs is ended (below, "The state file"). It listens at
$NBT_TMP_DIR/channels/$NBT_CHANNEL (removing a file an earlier run left there; a backlog of 64, since on QNX a
connect to a full queue is refused), writes the state file, begins to end what the state file of a relay that died
names, and removes every keys folder left in $NBT_TMP_DIR/auth (the folder auth itself, made again at once,
0700). Then the host key, $NBT_DATA_DIR/hostkey_ed25519: when it is not there it is made with the key tool
($NBT_APP_DIR/bin/dropbearmulti dropbearkey -t ed25519 -f FILE) and set to 0600 (the tool also writes the public
line to FILE.pub, which the relay leaves where it is); its public line is read with the key tool
(dropbearkey -y -f FILE), without the tool's comment. Apart from the end of an older relay, this is the
one thing the relay waits for (at most 20 s for each run of the tool), and it comes after the socket is listened
on, so the
agent's 5 s for the socket to appear are not spent on it; connections meanwhile wait in the queue. When the key
cannot be made or read, the relay says so and answers every connection failed. The key is made once and kept:
it goes with the package's data.

A connection:

  1. The agent's line of JSON and its newline, at most 2,048 bytes: {"v":1,"channel":"ssh","access":"both",
     "caller":"nbtapps"|"cli","phone":NAME,"maxMessage":4096}. The relay keeps caller, phone (which may be absent)
     and maxMessage (256 to 65,535, so that the answer fits one message). After it, each message either way is two bytes of length, high byte first, and
     1 to maxMessage bytes. A line that cannot be read, or a message of another length from the agent, ends the
     connection with nothing sent.
  2. The phone's first message, which has to come within 10 s of the connection or the connection is dropped:
       { "v": 1, "keys": [ "LINE", ... ] }
     v is 1; keys holds 1 to 8 key lines, each of which passes the rule below. Other members are not looked at.
     Anything else (not JSON, another v, no list, a line that fails) is answered bad-keys.
  3. With four connections that have, or wait for, a server, the answer is too-many; with no host key, failed.
     When the pty driver is to be started, or is being looked for, the connection waits for that (below), at most
     2 s; its 10 s for the keys message are over once the message has come. What the phone sends behind the keys
     message meanwhile waits in the relay.
  4. The lines are written, each with a newline, to $NBT_TMP_DIR/auth/<n>/authorized_keys, where n is the
     connection's number in this run: the folder 0700, the file 0600, on the RAM disk. (A folder of that number
     that a relay which did not end its connections left is removed first.)
  5. The server is started (below), and the answer is one message:
       { "v": 1, "hostKeys": [ "ssh-ed25519 AAAA..." ], "pty": true }
     or, with nothing started and nothing left, { "error": CODE } and the connection closed once it has gone:
       bad-keys   the keys message is not as above
       too-many   four connections have, or wait for, a server
       failed     no host key, the keys folder or file cannot be made, or the server cannot be started
     There is no error for want of a pty: copying files and running single commands need none, so a connection
     is served with "pty": false all the same (below).
  6. Then the bridge. What the server writes goes to the agent in messages of up to maxMessage bytes, cut where
     the reads fall; the phone's messages go to the server as bytes, their boundaries ignored. Each direction
     holds about one message (up to 2 + maxMessage bytes from the agent, maxMessage for the server, one framed
     message for the agent), and a side is not read while what it sent before has not been taken: a slow reader
     on either side holds the other back, and the relay's memory stays bounded.

The key-line rule. A line is TYPE SP BASE64, or TYPE SP BASE64 SP COMMENT:
  - TYPE is ssh-ed25519 or ecdsa-sha2-nistp256, and comes first, so that no line can put authorized_keys options
    (command="...", no-pty, from=...) in front of its key;
  - BASE64 is strict base64 (standard alphabet, padded, no bits left over) of an SSH public key blob whose first
    string is TYPE and whose rest is exactly that type's key: a string of 32 bytes for ssh-ed25519; the string
    nistp256 and an uncompressed point of 65 bytes for ecdsa-sha2-nistp256. RSA and other types are refused;
  - COMMENT is 1 to 128 characters;
  - every byte is printable ASCII, space to tilde: no control character, tab, line end or byte outside ASCII;
    so a line is at most 289 bytes.

The server. A socket pair; one end is the server's standard input and output, its standard error is the relay's
(so its -E lines are in the channel's log), and it gets no other file of the relay's. It is started as

  $NBT_APP_DIR/bin/dropbearmulti dropbear -i -E -s -e -j -k -I 1800 -r HOSTKEY -D KEYSFOLDER

one connection on its standard input (-i), its log to standard error (-E), no password logins (-s), the
environment passed on to what it runs (-e), no port forwarding either way (-j, -k), ended after 30 minutes with no
traffic (-I 1800). It leads a session of its own, so the agent's signals to the relay's process group do not reach
it and what it starts can be found by session; no signal is blocked in it, and SIGPIPE, SIGHUP, SIGINT, SIGTERM and
SIGCHLD are at their defaults. Its environment is the relay's making, with nothing of the relay's own: on QNX,
PATH=$NBT_APP_DIR/bin:/proc/boot:/boot/bin:/bin:/opt/os/bin:/opt/sys/bin:/opt/core/bin:/opt/car/bin and
LD_LIBRARY_PATH=/proc/boot:/boot/lib:/lib:/usr/lib:/opt/os/lib:/opt/sys/lib:/opt/core/lib:/opt/car/lib; elsewhere
PATH=$NBT_APP_DIR/bin and then the relay's own PATH. The package's bin/ comes first for sftp-server, and for scp,
which is a name of dropbearmulti. On QNX it is started with spawn(), given the list of its three files and
SPAWN_SETSID, as the agent starts its programs (no fork); elsewhere with fork(), setsid() and execve(), with a pipe
that the exec closes so that a program that cannot be started is known at once, as spawn() makes it known.

How a connection ends:
  - The agent's side closes (the phone closed the channel, the link dropped, the channel was switched off): the
    relay closes the server's end, and the server exits.
  - The server closes its end, or has exited and its end holds nothing more: what still waits for the agent goes
    first, then the agent's connection is closed.
  - Either way, then, what the server left behind is ended. A login shell on a pty leads a session of its own and
    its background jobs stay after it (each in a group of its own, in that session); without a pty, the shell and
    everything it ran stay in the server's own session. QNX has no call that signals a session. So while a server
    runs, the relay notes about once a second the server's children that lead a session (the login shells), and
    once more when the agent's side closes, while the server is still their parent. At the end it walks the
    process list and sends SIGHUP to every process group whose session is the server's (its pid) or a noted one,
    and 2 s later SIGKILL to each such group that is still there, without waiting meanwhile. It signals no group
    of another session, and never its own. A shell that a server starts in the last second before it ends by
    itself can be missed. The connection's keys folder is removed, by the path the connection made.
  - Ended children are collected as they end.
  - SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP to the relay: it stops listening and removes the socket file, ends every connection
    so, with the SIGKILL 0.5 s after the SIGHUP, ends its pty driver the same way (SIGTERM, SIGKILL 0.5 s later if
    it is still there), removes the keys folders (and auth/ when it is empty) and the state file, and exits with
    0, all by 1.5 s at most: within the 2 s the agent waits before its SIGKILL to the relay's group, which would
    not reach the servers' sessions or the driver's.

The pty driver. A shell needs a pty. On the head unit ptys come from /bin/devc-pty, a program of the car's own
debug set-up, which may not be installed, and may already run with ptys of its own. At a keys message, when the
relay has no driver of its own running and /bin/devc-pty is there and can be run, it runs `/bin/devc-pty -n 4`
(in a session of its own, its output to /dev/null, its errors to the relay's log). On QNX devc-pty detaches: the
relay is told its child has ended (waitpid gives an exit with 0) while the driver runs on under the pid it was
started with, in session 1, its parent still the relay; when it is ended later it leaves nothing to collect. A
devc-pty of another build could as well run on under a new pid that its command starts, so the relay takes
either. It takes the process list before it runs the command, reads the start time of the command's own process,
and then looks every 100 ms, for 2 s at most, at the processes that were not in that list, have not ended, have a
start time and run a program of the file name devc-pty (or of the file /bin/devc-pty leads to through links):
  - the command's own process (its pid with the start time it had) is taken once the relay has been told it ended
    and it still runs, or at the end of the 2 s when it still runs;
  - another such process is taken when it is the only one and the command's own process does not run beside it.
    While two or more are there (a starter that has not gone yet beside its driver) the look goes on, and at the
    end of the 2 s none of them is taken: they are left alone.
The driver's start time is kept with its pid. Connections whose keys come meanwhile wait for the same look, and
when the driver is found, or the 2 s are over, or the command failed and none appeared, they all go on. The relay
never signals a devc-pty it did not find so: another one, the debug set-up's, is left alone whenever it started.
A start that found none is not tried again for 30 s; the connections meanwhile go on at once.
  - 30 s after the last connection has ended (when no connection is left that has not ended, also one still
    waiting for its keys message), the relay sends its driver SIGTERM, which removes the driver's names, and
    SIGKILL 2 s later if it is still there. A connection within the 30 s keeps it, and the 30 s start again
    after it. One whose keys come while a driver is being ended sends it its SIGKILL at once and starts another.
  - A driver that has gone by itself is noticed at the next keys message, or with the notes of the sessions
    (about once a second while a server runs), and one is started again at the next connection.
  - Before it signals its driver the relay checks that the pid still runs, with the start time it had, a program
    of the driver's file name.
  - Elsewhere than QNX there is no such driver and ptys are always there: nothing is started.

pty in the answer. "pty" is true when the relay's own driver runs, or, without one, when the system has ptys
anyway: on QNX when at least one name /dev/ptyp* is there (another driver's); elsewhere always. A connection is
never refused for want of a pty: scp, sftp and single commands need none. Nor does "pty": true promise one: the
server takes whichever pty is free on the whole system when a client asks for one, any one connection can ask for
several, and other programs use them too; with none free the server cannot give a session one. (Dropbear also
closes a session's pty when the client's input ends, so a client keeps its input open while a command runs on a
pty.)

Four connections at once. A keys message while four connections have, or wait for, a server is answered too-many
before anything is made for it, and the connection is closed. A slot is free again once a connection has ended:
its agent side closed, its server's end closed and the SIGHUP sent, not when the SIGKILL 2 s later is. So a phone
that loses its link and connects again at once is not refused while what its last server left is being ended;
the processes of four ended connections may meanwhile still be there for up to 2 s beside those of four new ones.

Its life. The relay does not exit by itself: with no connection only the driver goes, after its 30 s. The agent
ends it (SIGTERM to its group, SIGKILL 2 s later) when the channel is switched off or its package changes.

The state file, $NBT_TMP_DIR/sshrelay.state, for a relay that dies without its cleanup (SIGKILL, a crash). One
line of JSON, written whole whenever what it holds changes (a new file, renamed over the old one, so that a reader
never sees half of one), and removed at a clean exit:
  { "v": 1, "relay": REC, "driver": REC, "servers": [ { "pid": N, "start": "T", "logins": [ REC, ... ] } ] }
REC is { "pid": N, "start": "T" }, a pid and that process's start time as the process list gives it (QNX: ns, from
DCMD_PROC_INFO; Linux: clock ticks since boot), "0" when it was not known; "driver" is there while the relay has
one; "servers" holds each connection's server whose cleanup is not done, with the login sessions noted for it.
The start time is a string of decimal digits, not a JSON number: on QNX it has 18 digits or more, and the agent's
JSON reader, which the relay uses, takes no integer above 2^53 - 1. It is read back only in the form it is written
in: 1 to 20 digits, no leading zero, at most 2^64 - 1.
At a start:
  1. A file that cannot be read, or holds anything else (another key, another type, a pid of 1 or less, a start
     time in another form, more than 64 entries), is ignored whole and replaced: nothing it names is signalled.
  2. Before listening: if the relay it names still runs (the same pid and start time, not ended, and a program of
     this relay's file name), it is sent SIGTERM and waited for 2 s, then sent SIGKILL and waited for 0.3 s at
     most; the file is read again after it. A relay that ends cleanly removes the socket file and the state file;
     one that does not leaves the file for the next step. So the start's worst case before the socket is there
     is about 2.3 s (the 2 s, the 0.3 s, and two reads of the process list), inside the 5 s the agent waits.
  3. After listening, without holding the loop: what the file names is ended as a connection's leftovers are,
     SIGHUP to every process group of the sessions that pass the checks below and SIGKILL 2 s later to those
     still there, and the driver gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 2 s later (or 0.5 s at a SIGTERM to the relay).
     Never pid 1 or below, the relay's own pid, group or session.
       - A server's session (the server's pid) and a login session (its leader's pid): when a process of that pid
         is there and has not ended, it must have the recorded start time and still lead its session, and for a
         server run a program of the server's file name (dropbearmulti); otherwise the session is left alone.
         When no process of that pid is there, the session is ended only if processes are still in it.
       - The driver: the same pid and start time, not ended, and a program of the driver's file name.
     A session's id is its leader's pid, which is not given to another process while the session has members.
     So a process that is none of these is hit only if it has the recorded pid and start time, which makes it
     the recorded process, or if it is in a session whose leader was given the recorded pid after the recorded
     session had emptied and has itself ended since, all between the death of the relay and this start.
  4. Every keys folder in $NBT_TMP_DIR/auth is removed.

Its log is its standard error, which the agent appends to the channel's log (${tmp}/channels/ssh.log): a line for
its start (pid, socket), an older relay ended, what a relay that died left and what of it was ended, a state file
that was ignored, the host key (made, its SHA-256 fingerprint, or why there is none), each connection (#n
connected; the caller, the phone's name, and each key's type and comment; a wait for the driver; the server's pid
and whether there is a pty), a refusal and its code, how a connection ended (with the bytes each way and the
seconds), how its server exited, and what was ended after it (the groups given SIGHUP, and those still there for
SIGKILL); the driver started and its pid, or why none was; the driver gone by itself, sent SIGTERM and why, ended,
or sent SIGKILL. It never writes key material other than a key's type and comment, and never a message's bytes.
The server's own lines (logins, not what is typed) come between them, and the key tool's when it makes the key.

The test hooks. A build for this machine (make ssh-relay-host, -DNBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_HOOK) reads these variables;
the head unit's build has none of them, which make ssh-check checks (no NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_ in the program):
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_SERVER       a program run in the server's place, with the server's arguments after
                                 `dropbear`: the tests' stand-in (tests/ssh/standin_server.py), which echoes, reads
                                 or writes slowly, exits by itself, stays, or leaves processes behind as a login does
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_SERVER_NAME  the program name that program's processes have (a script's is its interpreter's),
                                 for the check a start makes before it ends a recorded server
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_PTY_DRIVER   a stand-in for /bin/devc-pty (tests/ssh/standin_pty.cpp, copied as devc-pty),
                                 treated as /bin/devc-pty is on QNX
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_DEV          a folder that stands for QNX's /dev where pty names are looked for; without it
                                 there are always ptys, as on any system but QNX
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_DRIVER_MS    the driver's 30 s, both of them (with no connection, and after a start that
                                 found none), in milliseconds
  NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_START_OFFSET a number added to every start time the process list gives (that is not 0), so
                                 that the state file holds start times of the size QNX's are

The tests, make ssh-test (tests/ssh/run_relay.py), on this machine and not in the bench's container: the key-line
rule (test_key_lines.cpp) and the walk of /proc with start times (test_processes.cpp); with stand-ins for the
server, the protocol (no keys message in time, an agent's line that cannot be read, bad keys messages, a good one:
the answer, the keys file, the modes, the server's arguments, files, session and environment; failed; the host key
made once), the bridge (1 MiB each way in messages of every size, compared; a slow reader on either side, with the
relay's memory looked at), and the ends (the client goes away; the server exits with bytes still to come; what a
server leaves in its session and in a session of its own ended, and a process of another session left; SIGTERM
with two connections open); with a stand-in for the driver too, in both forms (one that runs on under a new pid,
and one that is the process the relay started): the driver started at the first connection and its pid found,
not started for a second, kept by a connection within its time, ended after it and not before, started again,
ended at the relay's end, and another of the same name left alone; connections that wait for a driver that comes
late; a starter that stays beside its driver for a moment, not taken; one that never appears, and none there; one
that goes by itself; one that ignores SIGTERM; "pty" false with no ptys and the connection served, true with
another driver's names; four connections and a fifth too-many, and a slot free again after an end; with start
times of the size QNX gives (NBT_SSHRELAY_TEST_START_OFFSET): a relay killed with two servers, their leftovers and
a driver, and the next start ending them all and the keys folders, and serving; a state file naming unrelated
processes, and one of nonsense (also a start time in any form but the one written), signalling none, and start
times up to 2^64 - 1 read; an older relay still running ended before the new one listens,
also one that ignores SIGTERM, with the start's time measured; and, with this machine's Dropbear (make ssh-host)
and OpenSSH client, through tests/ssh/ssh_proxy.py as the client's proxy command: a login with a command and its
exit status, one on a pty, a wrong key refused, and the host key of the answer the one the client checks. With no
ssh on the machine those are skipped, and the last line says so. At the end no process of the tests may be left.


Provenance

The submodules are taken from GitHub, which is not where the releases are signed. Each tag's tree was compared,
once, with the release archive whose PGP signature had been verified:

  https://matt.ucc.asn.au/dropbear/releases/dropbear-2026.94.tar.bz2
    sha256 e098034a843699200c8c977a991fff73159735bf795d5f72ef672c41a6b1ae81
    signed by F734 7EF2 EE2E 07A2 6762 8CA9 4493 1494 F29C 6773 (dropbear-key-2015.asc on the same site)
  https://cdn.openbsd.org/pub/OpenBSD/OpenSSH/portable/openssh-10.6p2.tar.gz
    sha256 9ae44872090cf2f06b3cf9ff147e7f16f309671340763ed2f99248398ae29d5b
    signed by 7168 B983 815A 5EEF 59A4 ADFD 2A3F 414E 7360 60BA (RELEASE_KEY.asc)

Each archive, extracted, against `git archive` of the commit above, extracted: identical. Dropbear 967 files and
OpenSSH 893, the same names, the same bytes (diff -r) and the same modes, no links, nothing on one side only. So
the tags carry the generated files the archives carry (configure, config.h.in), and the build needs no autotools.
(Checked 2026-11-19.)

The same exports build the same bytes: two builds from two fresh exports gave identical stripped programs, for
the head unit and for this machine. The programs hold the paths of the source files they were compiled from (in
assertion messages): /src/build/ssh/src/... for the head unit, because the image is given headunit/ at /src, so
any checkout builds the same program there with the same image; for this machine, the checkout's own path, so
those differ from one checkout to another. Nothing holds a date.


Moving to a new upstream version

  1. In the submodule: git fetch --depth 1 origin tag NEWTAG, git checkout NEWTAG; then, in headunit/,
     git add ssh/dropbear (or ssh/openssh). That pins it.
  2. Compare the new tag's tree with its signed release archive as above, and write the result here.
  3. make ssh-src. A hunk of the patch that no longer applies fails here. Make the change again in a fresh
     export and write the patch with diff -ruN of the unpatched export against the changed one (paths a/ and b/,
     -p1), leaving out localoptions.h.
  4. Read the new src/default_options.h and sysoptions.h for options that localoptions.h has to set, and the
     CHANGES file for anything that touches -i, -D, -e or what QNX lacks.
  5. make ssh ssh-host ssh-check, and change the tags and commits at the top of this file.
  6. Look again at what the build compiles and links for the licences to ship (below), and raise the package's
     version (ssh/relay/version.hpp).


Licences to ship with the programs

Not kept here: tools/ssh_pkg.py takes them from the exported sources (build/ssh/src/) when it makes the package,
and puts them in its licenses/ folder with a README that says which program each belongs to. A notice is the
comments at the head of its file, up to its first line of code. The lists are of what the head unit's build
compiles and links: every object on dropbearmulti's link line that holds code, and the members of OpenSSH's two
libraries that sftp-server takes (found by the symbols each object defines that the program holds; checked
2026-11-20, for the pins above). For dropbearmulti:

  - dropbear/LICENSE (dropbear-LICENSE): Dropbear's MIT licence, and the notices of the parts from OpenSSH, PuTTY
    and TweetNaCl;
  - dropbear/libtomcrypt/LICENSE and dropbear/libtommath/LICENSE (libtomcrypt-LICENSE, libtommath-LICENSE);
  - in dropbear-notices, the notices at the head of these files, whose holders or terms LICENSE does not give in
    full: src/atomicio.c, src/chachapoly.c, src/compat.c (getusershell(), which QNX's libc has not), src/loginrec.c,
    src/mlkem768.c, src/libcrux_mlkem768_sha3.h (MIT, Cryspen; included by src/mlkem768.c), src/scp.c,
    src/scpmisc.c, src/sshpty.c, src/svr-authpubkey.c (its part from OpenSSH) and src/svr-authpubkeyoptions.c.
    src/fake-rfc2553.c, src/gcm.c, src/progressmeter.c and src/svr-authpam.c are compiled with their code switched
    off for the head unit, and src/sntrup761.c is public domain.
For sftp-server:

  - openssh/LICENCE (openssh-LICENCE), which collects the notices of OpenSSH's files and, in its part 8, those of
    the portable compatibility code;
  - in openssh-notices, the notices of the files it takes whose holders that file does not name: atomicio.c,
    misc.c, openbsd-compat/fmt_scaled.c, openbsd-compat/freezero.c, openbsd-compat/memmem.c,
    openbsd-compat/reallocarray.c, openbsd-compat/recallocarray.c, openbsd-compat/strtonum.c and platform-pledge.c.
    (openbsd-compat/explicit_bzero.c is public domain.)
A new upstream version, or other build options, can change what is compiled: look again (step 6 above), and change
the lists in tools/ssh_pkg.py with this one (make test-ssh-pkg holds the two together).


Worktrees

In a linked worktree, the submodules' clones live in the main repository's .git/worktrees/<name>/modules/
(`git rev-parse --git-dir` in a submodule shows it), not in the worktree's folder. `git worktree remove` refuses
a worktree with submodules: it takes --force, which also throws away uncommitted changes and untracked files, so
look at `git status` first. Such a worktree cannot be moved with `git worktree move` either.
